<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Nightly cycle for tegan-trades.

  Install:
    cp scripts/com.tseitz.tegan-trades.nightly.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.tseitz.tegan-trades.nightly.plist

  Run it once by hand without waiting for the schedule:
    launchctl kickstart -p gui/$(id -u)/com.tseitz.tegan-trades.nightly

  Remove:
    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.tseitz.tegan-trades.nightly

  Why launchd rather than cron or a Claude session: this must not depend on a terminal or an
  agent being open, and launchd runs a missed interval on wake — cron simply skips it.
  `claude -p` needs the GUI session's keychain for its subscription auth, which is why this is
  a LaunchAgent (gui/) and not a LaunchDaemon.

  **This polls; it does not schedule.** Every 120s launchd starts the script, which decides in
  ~50ms whether conditions are right and exits if not. All the policy — earliest hour, once a
  day, lid, battery — lives in the script's gate, where it can be read and changed in one
  place. See the gate comment in scripts/nightly.sh for why, and for the measurement that
  forced it.

  The short version: a `StartCalendarInterval` at 06:15 fires during DarkWake with the lid
  shut, and on battery the job then freezes and thaws seconds at a time until you open the lid
  — hours of wall clock for minutes of work, twice observed (2026-07-27, 2026-08-01). Nothing
  here wakes the machine on purpose: **a day the laptop never opens is a day the cycle does
  not run.** Deliberate — waking a sleeping laptop on battery to fetch transcripts is a worse
  trade than skipping the day.

  `caffeinate` moved into the script, below the gate. It can stop a machine falling asleep
  mid-run but cannot wake a sleeping one, and wrapping a 50ms poll would poke power management
  720 times a day for nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.tseitz.tegan-trades.nightly</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/tseitz/code/projects/tegan-trades/scripts/nightly.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/tseitz/code/projects/tegan-trades</string>

  <!-- The gate is a few `ioreg`/`pmset` reads, so a 2-minute poll costs well under a minute of
       CPU across a whole day. Slower would mean sitting down and waiting for the queue. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>120</integer>

  <!-- The script writes its own dated log; these catch anything that escapes it, including a
       failure to start at all — which is the case the script by definition cannot record. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/tseitz/code/projects/tegan-trades/data/logs/nightly/launchd.out</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/tseitz/code/projects/tegan-trades/data/logs/nightly/launchd.err</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <!-- launchd hands over a minimal PATH. uv and claude both live in ~/.local/bin; the script
         re-exports this too, so either alone is sufficient. -->
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/Users/tseitz/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/tseitz</string>
  </dict>

  <!-- Off: the job spends real money, so a crash-loop must not become a spend-loop. -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <false/>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>

  <!-- A stuck yt-dlp or a hung API call should not hold the slot until tomorrow. Generous:
       the observed full run is a few minutes, but a backfill after several quiet days is
       legitimately longer. -->
  <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
  <integer>3600</integer>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
